#edb/ref 17592186450523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450523} :answer-field/id #uuid "187f487c-26a4-5852-ba9e-dad42fa9e85d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e324ed91-4fae-5de5-89d5-cd26e5a4b312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164863} {:answer/id #uuid "740563d1-b2ae-5c42-94a8-321600325c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740563d1-b2ae-5c42-94a8-321600325c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164864} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38d805e-0745-5617-9a20-11b431455d74" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ddb02b-92cb-56e6-bef2-df860e70ca26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164867} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca0e3e6-336f-55ab-8f04-9f67ade95df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164868} {:answer/id #uuid "2f604df7-206c-51f7-9878-ccf3987ff08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164869} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f44a38-9c4d-555c-8e31-fc73981a0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca0e3e6-336f-55ab-8f04-9f67ade95df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164868} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace87305-ddfd-54fb-a584-0e03b68cf02b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efd96307-00da-56b8-bab1-563e900c1e1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340366" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1091f7e8-6330-5e59-9a2c-9f9973d68732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547352} {:answer/id #uuid "316390e2-a9a1-557a-b993-1eb5daa5337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547418} {:answer/id #uuid "513baa56-f91e-5c3b-99a2-c83dbfe5f274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547500} {:answer/id #uuid "95757a8f-1751-58fa-98e7-c089f0afadab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547645} {:answer/id #uuid "f962769d-467c-5c55-b93f-dae3d07918c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f962769d-467c-5c55-b93f-dae3d07918c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547825} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d9a9a1-d4bf-5f85-97bb-b57a4ffb6644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d11bc330-9efc-55d1-a388-de4f864cc23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340511" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,6)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+12)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,6).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+12)=f((x+6)+6)=f(x+6)=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(7)=f(1+6)=f(1)=F(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7)=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+3)=f(5)=F(5)=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+3)\\ne f(x)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8748773f-b48a-58ec-b951-7e2f13629f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397420} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3eeb27-01e0-5659-a89f-03c69d0adcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397421} {:answer/id #uuid "c5615bd6-2ce4-5da1-a40b-3086abdcadd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397422} {:answer/id #uuid "07510649-4bdf-56eb-8d9c-ed267705e564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397423} {:answer/id #uuid "03be0b57-cf22-512e-a317-32a2b0abe9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397424} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a9dc62-5426-5219-b618-40a8ef37dfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :a